Below is the complete TypeScript code for a plugin registry with a generic event bus, class decorator, and two example plugins. The code is organized into two parts: core types/registry (from Lane A) and decorator/plugins/usage (from Lane B). All TypeScript features are used as specified.

```typescript
// ============================================================
// Part 1: Core types and PluginRegistry (from Lane A)
// ============================================================

import React from 'react';

// Const enum for plugin status (Section 3.7 from source)
export const enum PluginStatus {
  PENDING = 'PENDING',
  ACTIVE = 'ACTIVE',
  ERROR = 'ERROR'
}

// Base event interface for generic event bus
export interface BaseEvent {
  eventType: string;
  timestamp: number;
}

// Component descriptor interface (Section 9.1, 9.4 from source)
export interface ComponentDescriptor {
  componentId: string;
  factory: () => Promise<React.ComponentType<any>>;
  metadata?: Record<string, unknown>;
}

// Plugin manifest interface (Section 9.1 from source)
export interface PluginManifest {
  name: string;
  version: string;
  dependencies: readonly string[];
  components: readonly ComponentDescriptor[];
}

// Decorator metadata key
export const PLUGIN_METADATA_KEY = '__plugin_manifest__';

// Generic PluginRegistry class with event bus type parameter (Section 10.1, 10.2 from source)
export class PluginRegistry<TEvent extends BaseEvent> {
  private manifests: Map<string, PluginManifest> = new Map();
  private eventHandlers: Map<string, Array<(event: TEvent) => void>> = new Map();

  constructor(private readonly eventBusName: string) {}

  register(pluginClass: Function): void {
    const manifest: PluginManifest | undefined = (pluginClass.prototype as any)[PLUGIN_METADATA_KEY];
    if (!manifest) {
      throw new Error(`Plugin class ${pluginClass.name} is missing @Plugin decorator`);
    }
    if (this.manifests.has(manifest.name)) {
      throw new Error(`Plugin ${manifest.name} already registered`);
    }
    this.manifests.set(manifest.name, manifest);
    console.log(`Registered plugin: ${manifest.name} v${manifest.version}`);
  }

  getManifest(name: string): PluginManifest | undefined {
    return this.manifests.get(name);
  }

  // Event bus methods (generic)
  on(eventType: string, handler: (event: TEvent) => void): void {
    if (!this.eventHandlers.has(eventType)) {
      this.eventHandlers.set(eventType, []);
    }
    this.eventHandlers.get(eventType)!.push(handler);
  }

  emit(event: TEvent): void {
    const handlers = this.eventHandlers.get(event.eventType);
    if (handlers) {
      handlers.forEach(handler => handler(event));
    }
  }
}

// ============================================================
// Part 2: Decorator, plugins, and usage (from Lane B)
// ============================================================

// Class decorator (Section 8.3 from source)
function Plugin(manifest: PluginManifest) {
  return function <T extends { new (...args: any[]): {} }>(constructor: T) {
    (constructor.prototype as any)[PLUGIN_METADATA_KEY] = manifest;
    return constructor;
  };
}

// Example component factories (dummy)
const AnalyticsDashboard: React.ComponentType<any> = () => React.createElement('div', null, 'Analytics');
const ReportView: React.ComponentType<any> = () => React.createElement('div', null, 'Report');

// Plugin 1: AnalyticsPlugin (no dependencies)
@Plugin({
  name: 'analytics',
  version: '1.0.0',
  dependencies: [],
  components: [
    {
      componentId: 'analytics-dashboard',
      factory: () => Promise.resolve(AnalyticsDashboard),
      metadata: { category: 'metrics' }
    }
  ]
})
class AnalyticsPlugin {
  // Plugin implementation (stub)
}

// Plugin 2: ReportingPlugin (depends on analytics)
@Plugin({
  name: 'reporting',
  version: '2.0.0',
  dependencies: ['analytics'],
  components: [
    {
      componentId: 'report-view',
      factory: () => Promise.resolve(ReportView),
      metadata: { category: 'reports' }
    }
  ]
})
class ReportingPlugin {
  // Plugin implementation (stub)
}

// Usage example
function main() {
  const registry = new PluginRegistry<BaseEvent>('main-bus');

  registry.register(AnalyticsPlugin);
  registry.register(ReportingPlugin);

  // Demonstrate event bus
  registry.on('plugin:loaded', (event) => {
    console.log(`Event received: ${event.eventType} at ${event.timestamp}`);
  });

  registry.emit({ eventType: 'plugin:loaded', timestamp: Date.now() });

  // Verify manifests
  const analyticsManifest = registry.getManifest('analytics');
  console.log(analyticsManifest?.name); // 'analytics'
  const reportingManifest = registry.getManifest('reporting');
  console.log(reportingManifest?.dependencies); // ['analytics']
}

main();
```

**Explanation of TypeScript features used:**
- **Interfaces (Section 9.1, 9.4):** `PluginManifest`, `ComponentDescriptor`, and `BaseEvent` define contracts for plugin structure and events. `ComponentDescriptor` uses optional `metadata` and a factory function returning a Promise.
- **Const enum (Section 3.7):** `PluginStatus` provides a fixed set of string literals for plugin state, inlined at compile time.
- **Generics (Section 10.1, 10.2):** `PluginRegistry<TEvent extends BaseEvent>` allows the registry to work with any event type that extends `BaseEvent`, enabling type-safe event handling.
- **Class decorator (Section 8.3):** The `@Plugin` decorator attaches manifest metadata to the class prototype, which the registry reads during registration.
- **Readonly arrays:** `PluginManifest.dependencies` and `components` use `readonly` to prevent mutation after definition.
- **Type constraints:** The generic parameter `TEvent` is constrained to `BaseEvent`, ensuring that all events have `eventType` and `timestamp`.

The two plugins demonstrate different dependency structures: `AnalyticsPlugin` has no dependencies, while `ReportingPlugin` depends on `'analytics'`. The registry's `register` method performs a runtime check for the decorator metadata, ensuring compile-time safety via the interface types.

